  {
   "id": "th1",
   "code": "CSE303",
   "title": "Why is aⁿbⁿ not regular but a*b* is?",
   "author": "Rahul M",
   "at": "2026-10-31T15:40:00.000Z",
   "votes": 14,
   "mine": false,
   "body": "Both have a’s followed by b’s. I don’t get why one needs a PDA and the other doesn’t.",
   "replies": [
    {
     "author": "Demo Teacher",
     "role": "faculty",
     "at": "2026-10-31T16:35:00.000Z",
     "votes": 21,
     "accepted": true,
     "body": "a*b* lets the counts differ, so the machine only needs to remember “am I still reading a’s?”. aⁿbⁿ forces equal counts, so it must remember how many a’s it saw: unbounded memory, which a DFA does not have. Try the pumping lemma with the string aᵖbᵖ."
    },
    {
     "author": "Sneha K",
     "role": "student",
     "at": "2026-11-01T03:00:00.000Z",
     "votes": 3,
     "body": "The pumping lemma part in Sipser chapter 1.4 explains this exact example."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "th2",
   "code": "CSE302",
   "title": "Is every BCNF relation also in 3NF?",
   "author": "You",
   "at": "2026-10-30T14:15:00.000Z",
   "votes": 6,
   "mine": true,
   "body": "Asking for the mid-term. My notes say yes but I want to be sure.",
   "replies": [
    {
     "author": "Teacher 8",
     "role": "faculty",
     "at": "2026-10-30T15:00:00.000Z",
     "votes": 9,
     "accepted": true,
     "body": "Yes. BCNF is stricter than 3NF, so any relation in BCNF is automatically in 3NF. The reverse is not always true."
    }
   ]
  },
